//   - Which of the user's accounts to use — the active context
//     (current_context), and nothing else. `entire auth use <name>` is the
//     single lever; the cluster's advertised cores only decide whether that
//     identity is accepted, never which identity is chosen.
//
// Account selection (selectContext) is therefore total: the active context is
// used when the cluster trusts its core, and every other case is an error.
//
// Two implicit tiers used to sit under the active context — "the sole eligible
// context" and an ambiguity error when several were eligible. Both are gone.
// They made the acting identity depend on what *else* happened to be stored, so
// adding a second login could silently change which account a command ran as,
// and the same command could act as different identities on two machines. For a
// question as consequential as "whose credentials is this operating under?",
// a predictable error beats a convenient guess: the user switches with
// `entire auth use` and the answer is always readable from current_context.
//
// We never fall back to an active context whose core does NOT front the
// cluster: the cluster would reject the exchanged token as "unknown
// cluster_host", and silently authenticating a staging identity against a
// prod cluster (or vice versa) is exactly the confusion the /.well-known
// lookup exists to prevent.
//
// debugf is optional; nil suppresses debug output.

